@* The portal's sign-out control, wherever it appears (issue #43). One click
   signs out, with no interstitial - but still by posting, which is the part
   that matters and the part the deleted confirm screen actually existed for.

   A bare GET endpoint is signed out by anything that merely fetches the URL -
   a link preview, a prefetching browser - so this is a real form submit
   carrying an antiforgery token, and AppRoutes.Logout answers POST only.

   Deliberately *not* a Blazor named form, unlike every other form in the
   portal. A named form posts back to the page it is rendered on and is
   dispatched by the component that declared it, and this control is rendered
   in the chrome: its page is whichever one the User happens to be looking at.
   Posting to one endpoint instead keeps the sign-out in one place and keeps
   it out of a circuit - SignOutAsync writes to the response, which needs an
   HttpContext an interactive render does not have (ADR 0003). *@

<form class="hub-signout" method="post" action="@AppRoutes.Logout">
    <AntiforgeryToken />

    @* aria-label only where there is no visible text to name the button -
       which is the nav, where the control is an icon. Setting it
       unconditionally was wrong: it overrides the accessible name, so the
       button reading "Sign out instead" on /change-password would have been
       announced as "Sign out", and a visible label has to be contained in
       the accessible name (WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name). Blazor omits a null
       attribute, so the call site that has text simply leaves it unset and
       the text becomes the name. *@
    <button type="submit" class="@Class"
            title="@Constants.Logout.ControlTitle"
            aria-label="@Label"
            aria-describedby="@Constants.Logout.ExplanationId">
        @ChildContent
    </button>

    @* The Explanation, for everyone rather than only a mouse. It is on the
       button's `title` too, but a tooltip needs a hover: a screen reader
       would never reach it, and a touch device has nothing to hover with.
       Described-by rather than a second label, because it is not what the
       control is called - it is the reassurance that signing out of the
       portal is not what stopped a Device syncing. *@
    <span id="@Constants.Logout.ExplanationId" class="hub-sr-only">@Constants.Logout.Explanation</span>
</form>

@code {
    // The look of the button, which is the call site's business: the nav wants
    // a round icon button on the gradient band, and a login card wants the
    // same understated link its other way-out wears.
    [Parameter]
    public string Class { get; set; } = "";

    // The accessible name, for a call site whose ChildContent is an icon and
    // so names nothing. Left null by a call site with visible text.
    [Parameter]
    public string? Label { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public RenderFragment ChildContent { get; set; } = default!;
}
